1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
Here's a herd of a
hundred Shorthorn cattle.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:06,480
Sixty-four are red,
thirty-two are roan, and four

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:07,200
are white.

4
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:10,480
From the co-dominance
video, you know each roan

5
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:13,280
carries one red allele
and one white allele.

6
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:15,760
So what fraction of all the coat colour

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,680
alleles in this herd are white?

8
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:19,200
Four per cent?

9
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:20,320
Thirty-six per cent?

10
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:24,080
Hold that thought, because
the answer is neither.

11
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:27,300
Start with the cattle themselves.

12
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:30,100
A frequency tells you
how common something is

13
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:31,780
in the group you counted.

14
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:34,580
It's the count divided by the total.

15
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,300
Sixty-four out of a
hundred cattle are red,

16
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:39,780
so the frequency of
red coats is sixty-four

17
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:40,900
per cent.

18
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:43,700
Roan is thirty-two per cent, and white is

19
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:44,580
four.

20
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:46,660
A table keeps the counts, and a bar

21
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:49,100
chart shows the pattern at a glance.

22
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:51,860
But always say what you divided by.

23
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:53,140
Here, it's cattle.

24
00:00:54,367 --> 00:00:55,486
Now the alleles.

25
00:00:56,047 --> 00:00:58,446
Every animal carries
two copies of the coat

26
00:00:58,527 --> 00:01:01,646
colour gene, so a hundred cattle hold two

27
00:01:01,727 --> 00:01:02,606
hundred copies.

28
00:01:03,247 --> 00:01:05,886
Each white cow has two
white alleles, which

29
00:01:06,007 --> 00:01:06,686
makes eight.

30
00:01:07,167 --> 00:01:10,046
Each roan has one, which
makes thirty-two more.

31
00:01:10,607 --> 00:01:13,486
That's forty white alleles
out of two hundred,

32
00:01:13,567 --> 00:01:15,726
so the white allele
frequency is twenty per

33
00:01:15,847 --> 00:01:18,766
cent, and the red allele is eighty.

34
00:01:18,847 --> 00:01:21,246
Four per cent counted
only the white cattle,

35
00:01:21,327 --> 00:01:24,526
and thirty-six counted
animals, not alleles.

36
00:01:25,734 --> 00:01:28,853
Cattle make this easy,
because every genotype looks

37
00:01:28,934 --> 00:01:29,413
different.

38
00:01:29,894 --> 00:01:30,853
Peas don't.

39
00:01:31,414 --> 00:01:33,493
In a field of a hundred pea plants,

40
00:01:33,574 --> 00:01:34,613
nine are short.

41
00:01:35,174 --> 00:01:38,053
Short is recessive, so
those nine must have

42
00:01:38,214 --> 00:01:39,573
two short alleles.

43
00:01:40,054 --> 00:01:42,613
That's a genotype
frequency of nine per cent.

44
00:01:43,254 --> 00:01:45,093
But a tall plant could be hiding a

45
00:01:45,174 --> 00:01:48,053
short allele, or not, and both kinds look

46
00:01:48,134 --> 00:01:48,693
the same.

47
00:01:49,174 --> 00:01:51,973
So looks alone can't count every allele.

48
00:01:52,374 --> 00:01:54,453
For that, you have to read the DNA

49
00:01:54,574 --> 00:01:55,253
itself.

50
00:01:56,467 --> 00:01:58,466
Line up the same stretch of DNA from

51
00:01:58,627 --> 00:02:01,266
several people, and most bases match.

52
00:02:01,507 --> 00:02:03,906
But at some positions,
people differ by just

53
00:02:04,147 --> 00:02:04,946
one base.

54
00:02:05,507 --> 00:02:08,386
Here, some copies have C, and others have

55
00:02:08,547 --> 00:02:08,946
T.

56
00:02:09,587 --> 00:02:13,346
That's a single nucleotide
polymorphism, or snip for

57
00:02:13,427 --> 00:02:13,826
short.

58
00:02:14,547 --> 00:02:18,546
Single nucleotide means one
base, and polymorphism means

59
00:02:18,627 --> 00:02:20,626
more than one form in the population.

60
00:02:21,107 --> 00:02:22,706
Each form is an allele.

61
00:02:23,267 --> 00:02:26,226
And because both copies
are read, every genotype

62
00:02:26,307 --> 00:02:29,746
shows directly, including the
heterozygous one, with one

63
00:02:29,907 --> 00:02:31,346
C and one T.

64
00:02:32,567 --> 00:02:35,606
Two unrelated people
differ at roughly one base

65
00:02:35,687 --> 00:02:38,566
in every thousand, so each of us carries

66
00:02:38,647 --> 00:02:40,326
millions of snips.

67
00:02:40,407 --> 00:02:42,566
Most sit in DNA that doesn't code for

68
00:02:42,607 --> 00:02:45,686
a protein, and change nothing we can see.

69
00:02:45,767 --> 00:02:47,446
A few do matter.

70
00:02:47,487 --> 00:02:49,606
The sickle cell allele
from the proteins video

71
00:02:49,687 --> 00:02:53,046
is one: a single A changed to T.

72
00:02:53,127 --> 00:02:55,366
So snips are markers we can count, and

73
00:02:55,447 --> 00:02:57,206
only sometimes causes.

74
00:02:58,434 --> 00:03:00,713
Now count them, just like the cattle.

75
00:03:00,994 --> 00:03:03,313
Fifty people are tested at this snip.

76
00:03:03,394 --> 00:03:06,913
Eighteen have C on both
copies, twenty-four have

77
00:03:06,994 --> 00:03:09,633
one C and one T, and eight have

78
00:03:09,794 --> 00:03:11,233
T on both.

79
00:03:11,274 --> 00:03:13,873
Fifty people carry a hundred copies.

80
00:03:13,954 --> 00:03:16,753
The T alleles come to
twenty-four, plus sixteen,

81
00:03:16,834 --> 00:03:18,193
which makes forty.

82
00:03:18,234 --> 00:03:20,593
So the T allele frequency is forty per

83
00:03:20,754 --> 00:03:21,473
cent.

84
00:03:21,634 --> 00:03:23,713
Count alleles, not people.

85
00:03:24,934 --> 00:03:27,813
Snips become evidence
when you compare groups.

86
00:03:28,374 --> 00:03:30,693
Say those fifty people
all have a particular

87
00:03:30,774 --> 00:03:33,653
disease, and fifty people
without it are tested

88
00:03:33,734 --> 00:03:34,133
too.

89
00:03:34,854 --> 00:03:37,333
In that second group, T is only twenty

90
00:03:37,414 --> 00:03:37,973
per cent.

91
00:03:38,454 --> 00:03:41,333
That's a trend: T turns up more often

92
00:03:41,414 --> 00:03:42,453
with the disease.

93
00:03:42,934 --> 00:03:44,773
We call that an association.

94
00:03:45,254 --> 00:03:47,733
But it doesn't prove that T causes it.

95
00:03:48,214 --> 00:03:50,013
The snip might just sit close to the

96
00:03:50,054 --> 00:03:53,093
DNA that really matters,
on the same chromosome.

97
00:03:53,654 --> 00:03:56,773
Close neighbours are rarely
split by crossing over,

98
00:03:56,854 --> 00:03:58,613
so they're inherited together.

99
00:03:59,834 --> 00:04:02,313
And one snip is only one marker.

100
00:04:02,794 --> 00:04:05,113
Two populations can share a version at one

101
00:04:05,194 --> 00:04:08,553
snip, and still differ
at thousands of others.

102
00:04:08,634 --> 00:04:11,833
That's why studies of
relatedness and ancestry compare

103
00:04:11,914 --> 00:04:13,833
many snips at once, and look at the

104
00:04:14,074 --> 00:04:15,113
overall pattern.

105
00:04:16,334 --> 00:04:18,253
Every one of these numbers comes from a

106
00:04:18,494 --> 00:04:20,973
sample, and samples have limits.

107
00:04:21,294 --> 00:04:23,453
Imagine a whole breed
where the white allele

108
00:04:23,534 --> 00:04:25,453
really is twenty per cent.

109
00:04:25,534 --> 00:04:27,693
Sample twenty cattle,
and you might get ten

110
00:04:27,774 --> 00:04:29,613
per cent, or thirty.

111
00:04:29,774 --> 00:04:32,493
Sample two hundred, and
you'll usually stay close

112
00:04:32,574 --> 00:04:33,613
to twenty.

113
00:04:33,694 --> 00:04:36,173
Small samples bounce around.

114
00:04:36,254 --> 00:04:39,373
A sample also has to
represent the population:

115
00:04:39,454 --> 00:04:42,573
one farm, or one family, may not.

116
00:04:42,694 --> 00:04:45,213
And everyone has to sort the same way.

117
00:04:45,534 --> 00:04:47,133
Is a red cow with a few white

118
00:04:47,214 --> 00:04:49,133
hairs red, or roan?

119
00:04:50,334 --> 00:04:54,893
Three words to take away:
frequency, snip, limits.

120
00:04:55,534 --> 00:04:58,253
Frequency is a count over a total, so

121
00:04:58,494 --> 00:04:59,733
say what you counted.

122
00:05:00,014 --> 00:05:02,333
A snip is a one-base difference that you

123
00:05:02,414 --> 00:05:03,933
can count in DNA.

124
00:05:04,174 --> 00:05:06,653
And limits remind you what a sample can't

125
00:05:06,734 --> 00:05:07,293
prove.

126
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:10,100
Two harder questions now.

127
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:13,940
First, a class samples
white clover plants at

128
00:05:14,020 --> 00:05:15,780
two sites on the school grounds.

129
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:18,340
Some plants have a pale V mark on

130
00:05:18,420 --> 00:05:19,940
their leaves, and some don't.

131
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:23,140
At site A, eighteen plants have the mark

132
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:24,740
and forty-two don't.

133
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:27,700
At site B, thirty have the mark and

134
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:28,580
thirty don't.

135
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:31,140
State the frequency of the leaf mark at

136
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:34,660
each site, compare the
sites, and identify one

137
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:36,100
limitation of the data.

138
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:37,940
Pause here to answer.

139
00:05:48,367 --> 00:05:50,286
Each site has sixty plants.

140
00:05:50,367 --> 00:05:53,646
Site A is eighteen out of sixty, or

141
00:05:53,887 --> 00:05:55,166
thirty per cent.

142
00:05:55,247 --> 00:05:57,886
Site B is thirty out of sixty, or

143
00:05:57,967 --> 00:05:59,166
fifty per cent.

144
00:05:59,247 --> 00:06:01,246
So the mark is more common in site

145
00:06:01,407 --> 00:06:02,486
B's sample.

146
00:06:02,767 --> 00:06:05,006
But sixty plants from one school is a

147
00:06:05,167 --> 00:06:07,966
small sample, and the
mark ranges from bold

148
00:06:08,047 --> 00:06:10,606
to very faint, so people can sort plants

149
00:06:10,687 --> 00:06:11,446
differently.

150
00:06:11,727 --> 00:06:14,686
If you divided eighteen
by forty-two, you used

151
00:06:14,767 --> 00:06:15,806
the wrong total.

152
00:06:17,034 --> 00:06:17,513
Second.

153
00:06:17,994 --> 00:06:20,393
Forty people are tested at a snip, where

154
00:06:20,434 --> 00:06:22,633
the base is either G or A.

155
00:06:22,954 --> 00:06:26,233
Twenty have G on both copies, sixteen have

156
00:06:26,314 --> 00:06:28,873
one G and one A, and four have

157
00:06:28,954 --> 00:06:29,993
A on both.

158
00:06:30,314 --> 00:06:33,353
Calculate the frequency of the A allele.

159
00:06:33,514 --> 00:06:36,073
Then, a study finds that A is more

160
00:06:36,194 --> 00:06:38,553
common in people with a certain disease.

161
00:06:38,634 --> 00:06:41,353
Does this show that A causes the disease?

162
00:06:41,474 --> 00:06:42,473
Explain.

163
00:06:42,634 --> 00:06:43,833
Pause here to answer.

164
00:06:54,234 --> 00:06:56,713
Forty people carry eighty copies.

165
00:06:56,954 --> 00:06:59,273
The A alleles are sixteen from the mixed

166
00:06:59,354 --> 00:07:02,313
genotypes, plus eight
from the four people with

167
00:07:02,434 --> 00:07:03,673
A on both copies.

168
00:07:04,154 --> 00:07:06,953
That's twenty-four out
of eighty, or thirty per

169
00:07:07,114 --> 00:07:07,753
cent.

170
00:07:07,834 --> 00:07:10,633
And no, it's an association, not proof.

171
00:07:11,114 --> 00:07:13,833
A might just be inherited
alongside the DNA

172
00:07:13,914 --> 00:07:15,193
that causes the disease.

173
00:07:15,834 --> 00:07:17,753
If you said ten per cent, you counted

174
00:07:17,994 --> 00:07:19,593
people, not alleles.

175
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:24,480
So, with frequency data:
say what you counted,

176
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:28,560
count alleles rather than
individuals, read the trend,

177
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:30,240
then name the limits.

178
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:33,120
And an association is a pattern, not a

179
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:33,760
cause.

180
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:37,760
Next, you'll collect and
present frequency data yourself.

181
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:40,880
Then we'll see how scientists read DNA, by

182
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:42,880
sequencing and profiling.

183
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:45,540
Here are the key notes.

184
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:47,700
Pause the video if you need more time

185
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:48,580
to copy them.
